course: ai
lesson: 17
title: Voice assistants and recommendations
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A voice assistant does two guesses in a row: first it turns the sounds it hears into words, then it guesses what you wanted those words to mean.
explain: When Grandma asks for "Dancing Queen" and gets a podcast about queens, one of those guesses slipped, just as a friend on a crackly phone line might mishear you.
explain: Video sites and games guess too: they watch what you click and how long you stay, then show more of the same, so one cat video can turn your whole page into cats.
explain: The same pattern-spotting helps unlock phones with faces, warn drivers who drift, help doctors read X-rays and help scientists work out the shapes of proteins.
explain: In every case it is a strong guess, not a certainty, so people still check, decide and stay in charge.
fact: Voice assistants turn sounds into words and then guess what you meant; accents and background noise can confuse them.
fact: For simple jobs with live data, such as timers and weather, voice assistants are usually useful.
fact: Recommendation systems learn from what you click and watch, and show you more of it; searching for new things on purpose widens what you see.
fact: Recommendations may push videos that keep people watching, including angry or scary ones.
fact: Some games use data about players to keep them playing longer, for example with well-timed rewards; deciding your stopping time before you start helps you stay in charge.
fact: Face recognition looks for patterns in faces, so very similar faces, such as identical twins, can sometimes fool it; adding a PIN makes a phone safer.
fact: Some cities test cars that drive themselves using cameras, sensors and AI; accidents have been reported and people argue about how safe they are.
fact: Many cars have helper systems, such as a beep when the car drifts out of its lane, but the driver is always responsible.
fact: AI can help doctors spot patterns in scans such as X-rays; doctors still check and decide.
fact: It was widely reported that scientists used AI to predict the shapes of proteins (tiny building parts inside every living thing), a breakthrough that helps research into medicines.
fact: Map apps guess traffic from data sent by many phones and can suggest a faster route.
fact: A map app can send many drivers down the same small lane and create a new jam there.
exercise: Grandma asks the speaker for "Dancing Queen" and gets a podcast about queens. Why? | It turned her words into a wrong guess about what she wanted | It decided on its own that the podcast was better for her | It does not have that song or any other song anywhere at all | It was playing a joke on Grandma
exercise: What is the best thing for Grandma to do? | Speak clearly, try again and laugh | Give up on the speaker for good | Shout as loudly as she can | Unplug it and plug it in again
exercise: Sam asks "What's the weather?" and gets the right answer. What is the assistant good at? | Simple jobs with live data, like weather and timers | Knowing exactly what Sam will do today | Deciding what Sam should wear outside today and tomorrow | Feeling whether it is cold or warm
exercise: Mia watches one cat video and her whole page fills with cats. Why? | The site learned from her click and shows more of the same | The site thinks cat videos are the best videos for every single person | Someone at the site chose cat videos for her | Her computer has caught a cat virus
exercise: What can Mia do so her page isn't only cats? | Search for new things on purpose | Watch even more cat videos to fill it up | Never click on any video ever again | Ask the site to choose everything for her
exercise: After one angry video Jake gets more angry videos. What is wise? | Notice how the videos make him feel, and switch off | Keep watching until the angry ones stop coming by themselves | Watch them faster so they end sooner | Reply angrily under every video he sees
exercise: Connor's game gives him a free reward just when he's about to quit. What might be happening? | The game may be designed to keep him playing | The game knows he is sad and wants to cheer him up | It is pure luck that happens to every single player | The game is saying sorry for being far too hard
exercise: How can Connor stop playing longer than he meant to? | Decide his stopping time before he starts | Wait for the game to tell him to stop | Play until he wins one more reward | Only play when the game sends him a gift or a prize
exercise: Lily's mum's phone opens for her twin cousin's face too. What does this show? | Face recognition matches patterns and can mix up very similar faces | The twin cousin must know mum's secret PIN | The phone opens for anyone in the same family, because families share faces | The phone is broken and must be thrown away
exercise: What makes Lily's mum's phone safer? | Using a PIN too for important things | Letting only twins hold the phone | Smiling every time she unlocks it | Turning face unlock on twice
exercise: Chloe sees a car driving itself through a city on the news. What is true? | Such cars are being tested, and people argue about how safe they are | Such cars never have accidents of any kind, so nobody worries about them | Every car in the world now drives itself | The car is really driven by a hidden person
exercise: Maddie's dad's car beeps when he drifts out of his lane. Who is in charge of the car? | Her dad, the driver | The beeping helper system | The company that made the car | Whoever is sitting in the back
exercise: AI helped doctors find a disease earlier in X-rays. Who makes the final decision? | The doctors, who check the AI's suggestion | The AI, because it is faster than doctors | The patient, by looking at the X-ray | Nobody, the machine prints a cure
exercise: Mia's teacher says AI predicted the shapes of proteins. Why does that matter? | Knowing protein shapes helps research into medicines | Proteins are the shapes of the food on your plate at dinner time | It means every disease is now cured | It shows AI can now do all of science
exercise: How does Sam's map app know about the traffic jam? | It uses data from many phones on the road | A helicopter watches every road for it | The drivers in the jam phone the app | It guesses from the colour of the sky
exercise: What can go wrong when everyone follows the map app? | Many cars squeeze into a small lane and make a new jam | The app runs out of roads to suggest, so the cars stop | All the traffic lights in the town turn red at once | The jam behind them disappears forever
test: "why does the speaker keep getting nan's songs wrong, is it deaf" Best answer? | It guesses words from sounds, and accents and noise trip it up | It hears perfectly well but just doesn't like her kind of music | It only understands young people's voices | It is deaf, speakers have no microphones
test: Your friend says, "My video page shows me what's good for me." What is closer to the truth? | It shows what keeps you clicking, which isn't always good for you | It checks every single video first to see if it is healthy for you | A doctor chooses the videos for each child | It shows the same videos to every person
test: What would happen if Jake only ever clicked angry videos? | His page would fill up with even more of them | The site would stop showing angry videos to him | His whole page would turn into cat videos instead | Nothing, clicks make no difference to the page
test: In a film, a car with a lane-beep system drives the family home while Dad sleeps. What does the film get wrong? | Helper systems only help; the driver must stay alert and in charge | Nothing, a lane-beep system can drive a car home all on its own | The beep should be louder to wake Dad up | Cars with helpers must be driven by children
test: "my twin opened my phone with her face, rubbish phone or what" Best reply? | Face unlock can mix up very similar faces, so add a PIN | The phone is fine, twins have identical brains | Phones always open for anyone who smiles | Your twin must have copied your fingerprints
test: Grandad says, "So the computer is the doctor now?" Which is right? | No, AI helps doctors spot things, and doctors check and decide | Yes, doctors only plug in the computer now and it does the rest | Yes, AI has replaced all hospital doctors | No, AI is never used in hospitals at all
test: Which of these is a game trick to keep you playing? | A surprise reward just when you were about to stop | A pause button you can press any time | A clock on the screen that shows how long you've played so far | A menu that lets you save and quit
test: The map app sends you down a tiny lane and it's jammed too. Why? | It sent lots of other drivers the same way | The lane was built to hold traffic jams on purpose | The app wanted you to see the pretty countryside | Tiny lanes are always faster than big roads
test: Which sentence about the protein discovery is careful and true? | It was widely reported as a big breakthrough for medicine research | It means AI has now cured every illness | It was only a rumour on the internet and never happened at all | It means scientists are no longer needed
test: Sam asks the speaker for the weather, then goes out in shorts though it is snowing. What did he forget? | To look out of the window as well | To ask the speaker the same thing a second time | To thank the speaker politely for its help | To turn the speaker up a bit louder
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a voice assistant? | It converts sounds into words and subsequently infers the intention of the speaker | It comprehends every utterance with complete accuracy | It replays recordings prepared in advance by human operators | It responds solely to the voice of the person who purchased it and to no other person
exam: It is given that a viewer, having watched a single video concerning cats, is subsequently presented with numerous similar videos. Identify the principal reason for this. | The recommendation system infers preferences from previous viewing | The site is obliged by law to promote animal content | Human staff select videos individually for each viewer | The viewer's device has been infected by malicious software
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a vehicle equipped with a lane departure warning. | Responsibility for the conduct of the vehicle remains with the driver | Responsibility passes entirely to the manufacturer upon activation of the system | The driver may lawfully cease to observe the road | The system is capable of conducting the journey unaided
exam: Ascertain the most accurate description of the role of artificial intelligence in the examination of radiographs. | It assists in the detection of patterns, the final determination remaining with physicians | It renders the attendance of physicians unnecessary | It is prohibited from use in all medical settings | It provides determinations that require no verification
